# scrub-patterns.txt.example
#
# Copy this file to scrub-patterns.txt and fill in YOUR private tokens.
# scrub-patterns.txt is gitignored and never committed.
#
# LINE FORMAT
#
#   [tag]... <regex>          # optional trailing comment
#
# The regex is everything after the leading tags and before an inline "#". It is
# a case-insensitive extended regex (grep -Ei). A match in any tracked file
# (except the excluded tooling files) makes scripts/scrub.sh exit 1.
#
# TWO TAGS. They exempt different checks and are not interchangeable. Both must
# LEAD the line: the trailing comment is stripped before the line is used as a
# regex, so a tag parked in the comment is stripped with it.
#
#   [allow-author]   Skips the pre-publish AUTHOR check only.
#                    Use it for a name you commit under: docs/CONTRIBUTING.md
#                    requires a real name on every DCO sign-off. The file scan
#                    still enforces the line, so the value is still blocked
#                    from appearing inside a tracked file.
#
#   [allow-public]   Skips the FILE SCAN only.
#                    Narrow escape hatch for a value that is public by
#                    construction and therefore expected in your own files: a
#                    GitHub handle appears in every clone and install URL in
#                    the README, and a copyright holder appears in LICENSE,
#                    NOTICE and AUTHORS. Do not use it for an address, a host,
#                    an IP, or anything you would mind seeing in a search
#                    engine. A value that is both (your own name, published in
#                    LICENSE) carries both tags.
#
# MIGRATION from 0.1.0 (the only release ever published): the tag used to sit in
# the trailing comment,
# and the file scan passed that comment straight to grep, so every line was a
# regex that matched nothing. Both scripts now strip the comment. If your
# scrub-patterns.txt still ends a line with "# ... [allow-author]", move the tag
# to the front of the line. Until you do, both scripts print a WARN naming the
# line, the author check stops exempting it, and the file scan starts enforcing
# it. Nothing is silently exempted.
#
# Usage:
#   cp scrub-patterns.txt.example scrub-patterns.txt
#   # Edit scrub-patterns.txt and replace each <placeholder> with your real value.
#   bash scripts/scrub.sh

# --- Server / infrastructure ---
<your-server-ip>           # e.g. 203\.0\.113\.1 (escape dots for regex)
/Users/<your-username>     # macOS home path leak

# --- Personal identity ---
# Tagged [allow-author] so your own DCO sign-off does not fail check (b), and
# deliberately NOT tagged [allow-public]: the file scan still enforces them.
# Add [allow-public] to a line only after the scan flags it in a file you are
# publishing on purpose.
[allow-author] <your-username>          # lowercase username (e.g. johndoe)
[allow-author] \b<YourFirstName>\b      # first name word-boundary match
[allow-author] \b<YourLastName>\b       # last name word-boundary match
<your-employer>            # company / school name

# --- Project codenames ---
<your-project-name>        # internal project names you don't want published
<your-prop-firm>           # e.g. FirmA|FirmB (pipe-separated alternatives)

# --- Secrets and tokens ---
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN\s*=\s*\S   # env-var assignment with a value
[A-Za-z0-9._%+-]+@(gmail|outlook|yahoo|hotmail|proton)[a-z.]+  # personal email

# --- Financial amounts (optional: flag large dollar amounts in text files) ---
# \$\s?\d{3,}

# --- Social / handle ---
# @<your-handle>
